@page "/springs"

<PageTitle>Springs · Bmotion</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Springs</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        A tween asks "how long should this take?". A spring asks "how heavy is this, and how hard is it
        pulled?" - and the duration falls out of the answer. That is why springs survive interruption:
        retarget one mid-flight and it carries its current velocity into the new motion instead of
        restarting. <strong>Bm.Spring(...)</strong> is the whole API, and it accepts either set of
        knobs below.
    </p>
</div>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Stiffness and damping</h3>
    <p>
        <strong>Stiffness</strong> is how hard the spring pulls toward the target - raise it and the
        motion gets faster and more urgent. <strong>Damping</strong> is the friction that removes
        energy - lower it and the element overshoots and wobbles, raise it far enough and it eases
        in without ever crossing the target. <strong>Mass</strong> (default <code>1</code>) scales
        the inertia behind both.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        @foreach (var cfg in _springs)
        {
            <div class="demo-card stack">
                <span class="demo-label">@cfg.Label</span>
                <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: 0))"
                        Transition="@cfg.Config">
                    <div class="box box-sm" />
                </Bmotion>
                <span class="demo-label">@cfg.Note</span>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="@(() => _on = !_on)">@(_on ? "Send back" : "Animate")</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_springCode" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Bounce and duration</h3>
    <p>
        Stiffness and damping are the physics, but they are not how anyone describes motion out
        loud. The second form takes the two words you would actually use: <strong>bounce</strong>
        (<code>0</code> = no overshoot at all, <code>1</code> = very springy) and
        <strong>duration</strong> in visual seconds. Bmotion solves for the constants that produce
        it, so the same feel is reachable without tuning numbers by trial.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        @foreach (var cfg in _bounces)
        {
            <div class="demo-card stack">
                <span class="demo-label">@cfg.Label</span>
                <Bmotion Animate="@(_bounce ? Bm.To(y: 60) : Bm.To(y: 0))"
                        Transition="@cfg.Config">
                    <div class="box box-sm" />
                </Bmotion>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="@(() => _bounce = !_bounce)">@(_bounce ? "Send back" : "Animate")</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_bounceCode" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Mass and a starting push</h3>
    <p>
        <strong>mass</strong> is the inertia the spring has to move. With the stiffness and damping held, a heavier
        element is slower to get going and swings further past the target before the same friction stops it.
        <strong>velocity</strong> is a push the element already has when the spring takes over, in units per
        second - a negative one sends it the wrong way first, which is how a recoil or a flick reads. Left at
        <code>0</code>, a spring that interrupts another animation inherits that animation's velocity instead (see
        below). Two more knobs, set on a <code>new BmSpring { … }</code>, decide when a spring counts as finished:
        <code>RestSpeed</code> and <code>RestDelta</code>, the speed and the distance from the target below which
        it stops. Raise them to cut a long, barely visible tail short.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        @foreach (var cfg in _masses)
        {
            <div class="demo-card stack">
                <span class="demo-label">@cfg.Label</span>
                <Bmotion Animate="@(_massed ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: 0))"
                        Transition="@cfg.Config">
                    <div class="box box-sm" />
                </Bmotion>
                <span class="demo-label">@cfg.Note</span>
            </div>
        }
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="@(() => _massed = !_massed)">@(_massed ? "Send back" : "Animate")</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_massCode" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Interrupting mid-flight</h3>
    <p>
        Change the target while the element is still on its way, and the two kinds of transition part company. A
        tween starts its new run from where the element is, but it has no notion of speed: an <code>InOut</code>
        curve eases in from a standstill, so the turnaround is a visible stop. A spring carries the velocity it
        has into the new motion and curves round without one. Press the button, then press it again before the
        boxes arrive, and watch each one turn around.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Bm.Tween(0.9, BmEase.InOut) &middot; stops, then turns</span>
            <Bmotion Animate="@(_chased ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: -80))"
                     Transition="Bm.Tween(0.9, BmEase.InOut)">
                <div class="box box-sm" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>

        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.15, duration: 0.9) &middot; curves round</span>
            <Bmotion Animate="@(_chased ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: -80))"
                     Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.15, duration: 0.9)">
                <div class="box box-sm" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="@(() => _chased = !_chased)">Retarget</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_interruptCode" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Overshoot on mount</h3>
    <p>
        An underdamped spring on an entrance is the cheapest way to make an interface feel alive:
        the element passes its resting state, comes back, and settles. Re-mount below to replay it -
        the <code>@@key</code> change is what makes Blazor treat it as a new element, which is what
        makes <code>Initial</code> run again.
    </p>
    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card">
            <Bmotion Initial="Bm.To(scale: 0, rotate: -180)"
                    Animate="Bm.To(scale: 1, rotate: 0)"
                    Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 260, damping: 12)"
                    @key="@_mountKey">
                <div class="box" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="Remount">Re-mount</button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_bouncyCode" />
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Repeating springs</h3>
    <p>
        <strong>BmRepeat</strong> replays a spring without an <code>int.MaxValue</code> sentinel
        anywhere in sight. <em>Mirror</em> ping-pongs back through the motion it just played;
        <em>Loop</em> jumps home and replays from the origin, optionally with a pause between runs. The count is
        the number of runs <em>after</em> the first, so <code>BmRepeat.Loop(3)</code> plays four times in all. The
        <a href="keyframes">Keyframes</a> page puts all three repeat types side by side.
    </p>

    <div class="demo-row">
        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Mirror &middot; forever</span>
            @* The repeat belongs to the run out only: a transition applies to whatever target it is given, so
               keeping it on the way back would mirror between here and home forever instead of resetting. *@
            <Bmotion Animate="@(_repeat ? Bm.To(x: 90) : Bm.To(x: 0))"
                    Transition="@(_repeat ? Bm.Spring(stiffness: 200, damping: 12, repeat: BmRepeat.Mirror())
                                          : Bm.Spring(stiffness: 200, damping: 12))">
                <div class="box box-sm" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>

        <div class="demo-card stack">
            <span class="demo-label">Loop(3) &middot; 3 more runs, 300 ms apart</span>
            <Bmotion Animate="@(_repeat ? Bm.To(y: 60) : Bm.To(y: 0))"
                    Transition="@(_repeat ? Bm.Spring(stiffness: 300, damping: 18, repeat: BmRepeat.Loop(3, delay: 0.3))
                                          : Bm.Spring(stiffness: 300, damping: 18))">
                <div class="box box-sm" />
            </Bmotion>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-toolbar">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="@(() => _repeat = !_repeat)">
            @(_repeat ? "Reset" : "Start repeating")
        </button>
    </div>

    <CodeSnippet Code="@_repeatCode" />
</section>

@code {
    private bool _on;
    private bool _bounce;
    private bool _repeat;
    private bool _massed;
    private bool _chased;
    private int  _mountKey;

    /// <summary>One spring stage: its caption, the transition it plays and a note on the feel.</summary>
    private record SpringDemo(string Label, BmTransition Config, string Note = "");

    private readonly SpringDemo[] _springs =
    [
        new("stiffness 400 · damping 40", Bm.Spring(400, 40), "fast, no overshoot"),
        new("stiffness 100 · damping 10", Bm.Spring(100, 10), "the default feel"),
        new("stiffness 50 · damping 5",   Bm.Spring(50,  5),  "loose and wobbly"),
        new("stiffness 300 · damping 70", Bm.Spring(300, 70), "overdamped - it crawls in"),
    ];

    private readonly SpringDemo[] _bounces =
    [
        new("bounce 0 · duration 0.6",   Bm.Spring(bounce: 0,    duration: 0.6)),
        new("bounce 0.3 · duration 0.6", Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.3,  duration: 0.6)),
        new("bounce 0.6 · duration 0.6", Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.6,  duration: 0.6)),
        new("bounce 0.6 · duration 1.2", Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.6,  duration: 1.2)),
    ];

    private readonly SpringDemo[] _masses =
    [
        new("mass 1",           Bm.Spring(stiffness: 100, damping: 10, mass: 1),   "the default"),
        new("mass 4",           Bm.Spring(stiffness: 100, damping: 10, mass: 4),   "slower, swings further"),
        new("velocity: -600",   Bm.Spring(stiffness: 100, damping: 12, velocity: -600), "pushed the wrong way first"),
    ];

    void Remount() => _mountKey++;

    private const string _massCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* Same stiffness and damping: only the mass changes, so only the inertia does. *@
        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: 0))"
                 Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 100, damping: 10, mass: 4)">
            <div class="box-sm" />
        </Bmotion>

        @* A starting push in units per second; negative moves it away from the target first. *@
        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: 0))"
                 Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 100, damping: 12, velocity: -600)">
            <div class="box-sm" />
        </Bmotion>

        @* The default spring, told to call itself settled sooner. *@
        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: 0))" Transition="_quickToSettle">
            <div class="box-sm" />
        </Bmotion>

        <button @onclick="@(() => _on = !_on)">Animate</button>

        @code {
            private bool _on;

            // The two thresholds that end a spring live on the class: below this speed AND this
            // distance from the target, it counts as settled. The defaults are 0.01 for both.
            private readonly BmTransition _quickToSettle = new BmSpring
            {
                Stiffness = 100, Damping = 10, RestSpeed = 2, RestDelta = 0.5,
            };
        }
        """;

    private const string _interruptCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* A tween restarts from where the element is, but from a standstill... *@
        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: -80))"
                 Transition="Bm.Tween(0.9, BmEase.InOut)">
            <div class="box-sm" />
        </Bmotion>

        @* ...while a spring keeps the velocity it had, so a retarget curves round instead of
           stopping. Same visual duration as the tween, so both are at full speed mid-way.
           (An authored velocity: on the spring replaces the inherited one.) *@
        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: -80))"
                 Transition="Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.15, duration: 0.9)">
            <div class="box-sm" />
        </Bmotion>

        @* Press it twice, quickly. *@
        <button @onclick="@(() => _on = !_on)">Retarget</button>

        @code {
            private bool _on;
        }
        """;

    private const string _springCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        <style>
            :root { --bm-primary: #1276C6; --bm-primary-text: #fff; --bm-secondary: #FD7F36; --bm-text: #1A1A1A; --bm-muted: #525252; --bm-card: #F5F5F5; --bm-track: #EBEBEB; --bm-border: #E0E0E0; --bm-radius: 2px; --bm-accent-soft: rgba(18,118,198,.12); --bm-accent-line: rgba(18,118,198,.35); }
            .demo-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
            .demo-section h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: .5rem; }
            .demo-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.5rem; align-items: center; }
            .demo-card {
                background: var(--bm-card); border: 1px solid var(--bm-border);
                border-radius: var(--bm-radius); padding: 2rem; min-height: 160px; flex: 1;
                min-width: 220px; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .75rem;
                align-items: center; justify-content: center;
            }
            .label { font-size: .78rem; color: var(--bm-muted); }
            .box-sm {
                width: 50px; height: 50px; border-radius: 8px;
                background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bm-primary), var(--bm-secondary));
            }
            .btn-bm {
                background: var(--bm-primary); color: var(--bm-primary-text); border: none; border-radius: 8px;
                padding: .6rem 1.4rem; font-weight: 600; cursor: pointer;
            }
        </style>

        <div class="demo-section">
            <h2>Stiffness and damping</h2>

            <div class="demo-row">
                @foreach (var cfg in _springs)
                {
                    <div class="demo-card">
                        <span class="label">@cfg.Label</span>
                        <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(x: 80) : Bm.To(x: 0))"
                                Transition="@cfg.Config">
                            <div class="box-sm" />
                        </Bmotion>
                    </div>
                }
            </div>

            <button class="btn-bm" style="margin-top:1rem" @onclick="@(() => _on = !_on)">
                Animate
            </button>
        </div>

        @code {
            private bool _on;

            private record SpringDemo(string Label, BmTransition Config);

            private readonly SpringDemo[] _springs =
            [
                new("stiffness 400 · damping 40", Bm.Spring(400, 40)),   // fast, no overshoot
                new("stiffness 100 · damping 10", Bm.Spring(100, 10)),   // the default feel
                new("stiffness 50 · damping 5",   Bm.Spring(50,  5)),    // loose and wobbly
                new("stiffness 300 · damping 70", Bm.Spring(300, 70)),   // overdamped - it crawls in
            ];
        }
        """;

    private const string _bounceCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        @* The same spring, described the way a designer would: how bouncy, and for how long.
           Bmotion solves for the stiffness/damping/mass that produce it. *@
        @foreach (var bounce in _bounces)
        {
            <Bmotion Animate="@(_on ? Bm.To(y: 60) : Bm.To(y: 0))" Transition="bounce">
                <div class="box-sm" />
            </Bmotion>
        }

        <button @onclick="@(() => _on = !_on)">@(_on ? "Send back" : "Animate")</button>

        @code {
            private bool _on;

            private readonly BmTransition[] _bounces =
            [
                Bm.Spring(bounce: 0,   duration: 0.6),   // eases in, never crosses the target
                Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.3, duration: 0.6),   // a hint of overshoot
                Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.6, duration: 0.6),   // clearly springy
                Bm.Spring(bounce: 0.6, duration: 1.2),   // as springy, twice as long
            ];
        }
        """;

    private const string _bouncyCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        <style>
            :root { --bm-primary: #1276C6; --bm-primary-text: #fff; --bm-secondary: #FD7F36; --bm-text: #1A1A1A; --bm-muted: #525252; --bm-card: #F5F5F5; --bm-track: #EBEBEB; --bm-border: #E0E0E0; --bm-radius: 2px; --bm-accent-soft: rgba(18,118,198,.12); --bm-accent-line: rgba(18,118,198,.35); }
            .demo-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
            .demo-section h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: .5rem; }
            .demo-section p { color: var(--bm-muted); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.6; }
            .demo-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.5rem; align-items: center; }
            .demo-card {
                background: var(--bm-card); border: 1px solid var(--bm-border);
                border-radius: 12px; padding: 2rem; min-height: 160px; flex: 1; min-width: 220px;
                display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
            }
            .box {
                width: 80px; height: 80px; border-radius: 10px;
                background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bm-primary), var(--bm-secondary));
            }
            .btn-bm {
                background: var(--bm-primary); color: var(--bm-primary-text); border: none; border-radius: 8px;
                padding: .6rem 1.4rem; font-weight: 600; cursor: pointer;
            }
        </style>

        <div class="demo-section">
            <h2>Overshoot on mount</h2>
            <p>An underdamped spring passes the resting state, comes back, and settles.</p>
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-card">
                    @* @key is what makes Blazor treat this as a new element - which is what
                       makes Initial run again. *@
                    <Bmotion Initial="Bm.To(scale: 0, rotate: -180)"
                            Animate="Bm.To(scale: 1, rotate: 0)"
                            Transition="Bm.Spring(stiffness: 260, damping: 12)"
                            @key="@_mountKey">
                        <div class="box" />
                    </Bmotion>
                </div>
            </div>
            <button class="btn-bm" style="margin-top:1rem" @onclick="Remount">Re-mount</button>
        </div>

        @code {
            private int _mountKey;

            void Remount() => _mountKey++;
        }
        """;

    private const string _repeatCode = """
        @using Bit.Bmotion

        <style>
            :root { --bm-primary: #1276C6; --bm-primary-text: #fff; --bm-secondary: #FD7F36; --bm-text: #1A1A1A; --bm-muted: #525252; --bm-card: #F5F5F5; --bm-track: #EBEBEB; --bm-border: #E0E0E0; --bm-radius: 2px; --bm-accent-soft: rgba(18,118,198,.12); --bm-accent-line: rgba(18,118,198,.35); }
            .demo-section { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
            .demo-section h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 700; margin-bottom: .5rem; }
            .demo-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.5rem; align-items: center; }
            .demo-card {
                background: var(--bm-card); border: 1px solid var(--bm-border);
                border-radius: 12px; padding: 2rem; min-height: 160px; flex: 1; min-width: 220px;
                display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .75rem;
                align-items: center; justify-content: center;
            }
            .label { font-size: .78rem; color: var(--bm-muted); }
            .box-sm {
                width: 50px; height: 50px; border-radius: 8px;
                background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--bm-primary), var(--bm-secondary));
            }
            .btn-bm {
                background: var(--bm-primary); color: var(--bm-primary-text); border: none; border-radius: 8px;
                padding: .6rem 1.4rem; font-weight: 600; cursor: pointer;
            }
        </style>

        <div class="demo-section">
            <h2>Repeating springs</h2>

            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-card">
                    <span class="label">Mirror · forever</span>
                    @* Mirror ping-pongs back through the motion it just played. The repeat goes on the run
                       out only: a transition applies to whatever target it is handed, so one kept for the
                       way back would mirror between here and home forever. *@
                    <Bmotion Animate="@(_repeat ? Bm.To(x: 90) : Bm.To(x: 0))"
                            Transition="@(_repeat ? Bm.Spring(stiffness: 200, damping: 12, repeat: BmRepeat.Mirror())
                                                  : Bm.Spring(stiffness: 200, damping: 12))">
                        <div class="box-sm" />
                    </Bmotion>
                </div>

                <div class="demo-card">
                    <span class="label">Loop(3) · 3 more runs, 300 ms apart</span>
                    @* Loop jumps home and replays from the origin. The count is the runs AFTER the
                       first, so Loop(3) plays four times in all; repeat: 3 is the same as Loop(3). *@
                    <Bmotion Animate="@(_repeat ? Bm.To(y: 60) : Bm.To(y: 0))"
                            Transition="@(_repeat ? Bm.Spring(stiffness: 300, damping: 18, repeat: BmRepeat.Loop(3, delay: 0.3))
                                                  : Bm.Spring(stiffness: 300, damping: 18))">
                        <div class="box-sm" />
                    </Bmotion>
                </div>
            </div>

            <button class="btn-bm" style="margin-top:1rem" @onclick="@(() => _repeat = !_repeat)">
                @(_repeat ? "Reset" : "Start repeating")
            </button>
        </div>

        @code {
            private bool _repeat;
        }
        """;
}
